{"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/HMTask\/documentation\/HMTask"]]},"primaryContentSections":[{"content":[{"level":2,"type":"heading","anchor":"","text":""},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"These are, with the exceptions detailed below, asynchronous versions of exactly what you’ll find in "},{"isActive":true,"identifier":"https:\/\/catterwaul.github.io\/HemiprocneMystaceaResult\/documentation\/hmresult\/mapping-methods","type":"reference"},{"text":".","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"level":3,"text":"flatMap \/ map","type":"heading","anchor":"flatMap--map"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"With a synchronous wrapper, like "},{"code":"Result","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", a success-mapping transformation can throw a different type of error than the wrapper’s own error type.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"You can still throw two different error types, when mapping ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Task"},{"type":"text","text":"s. But there’s no way to preserve either of the error types, in the resulting "},{"code":"Task","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":", in doing so.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Mapping a "},{"code":"Task","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" is synchronous—it’s just storing a transformation function for later use. But waiting for a ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Task"},{"type":"text","text":" to be evaluated, to see if it throws an error, is asynchronous. Mapping methods have to handle the possibility of the "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Task"},{"type":"text","text":" failing, or the transformation failing. You’ve got to be able to deal with both the unmapped, and mapped, error types. This requires the transformed "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Task"},{"type":"text","text":"’s error type to be "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"any Error"},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"There is an exception:"}],"type":"strong"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"If the original ","type":"text"},{"code":"Task","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"’s error type is "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Never"},{"type":"text","text":", the "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Error"},{"type":"text","text":" type of the transformation could, in theory, match the transformed "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Task"},{"type":"text","text":"’s."}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"In practice, this is possible, but only when the transformation also ","type":"text"},{"type":"emphasis","inlineContent":[{"text":"doesn’t","type":"text"}]},{"text":" throw. ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Never"},{"text":" can be preserved in the transformed ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Task"},{"text":".","type":"text"}]},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The reason for this is that, unfortunately, while "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Task"},{"text":"s technically can use any type of error, there is no way to create a ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Task"},{"type":"text","text":" whose "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Failure"},{"type":"text","text":" is anything except "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Never"},{"type":"text","text":" or "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"any Error"},{"type":"text","text":". Hopefully this limitation will be lifted soon."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"type":"heading","level":3,"text":"flatMapAndMergeError \/ mapAndMergeError","anchor":"flatMapAndMergeError--mapAndMergeError"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"In theory, these would also be exceptions. An error type should be able to be preserved, if only one is used across the transformation."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"But for now, “merging error” overloads are not possible. You can perform the transformation, but the error type will be erased.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"anchor":"flatMapFailure--mapFailure","text":"flatMapFailure \/ mapFailure","type":"heading","level":3},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"In the future, these may be able to provide a true mapping to new "},{"code":"Task","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" type, with a typed "},{"code":"Failure","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":". For now, it at least can still transform an error—the result just needs to be erased to "},{"code":"any Error","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"anchor":"mapFailureToSuccess--mapFailureToSuccessAndErrorToFailure","text":"mapFailureToSuccess \/ mapFailureToSuccessAndErrorToFailure","type":"heading","level":3},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Only an overload with a non-throwing transformation is provided, for ","type":"text"},{"code":"mapFailureToSuccess","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":". Unlike with "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Result"},{"type":"text","text":", the only other option for handling transformation errors is "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"mapFailureToSuccessAndErrorToFailure"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"type":"paragraph"}],"kind":"content"}],"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/HMTask\/documentation\/HMTask\/Mapping-Methods"},"sections":[],"metadata":{"role":"collectionGroup","roleHeading":"API Collection","title":"Mapping Methods","modules":[{"name":"HMTask"}]},"kind":"article","topicSections":[{"anchor":"Topics","identifiers":["doc:\/\/HMTask\/documentation\/HMTask\/_Concurrency\/Task\/flatMap(_:)-3ogpm","doc:\/\/HMTask\/documentation\/HMTask\/_Concurrency\/Task\/map(_:)-8wbux"]},{"title":"Failure == Never","identifiers":["doc:\/\/HMTask\/documentation\/HMTask\/_Concurrency\/Task\/flatMap(_:)-8cxrg","doc:\/\/HMTask\/documentation\/HMTask\/_Concurrency\/Task\/map(_:)-4tunf"],"anchor":"Failure-==-Never"},{"title":"Failure != Never","identifiers":["doc:\/\/HMTask\/documentation\/HMTask\/_Concurrency\/Task\/flatMapFailure(_:)","doc:\/\/HMTask\/docum